     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 5,100 mg/day is about twice the maximum daily dose of metformin, and confirming it doesn't lower the risk to the patient.\n- **The order needs to come from the patient's prescriber.** Only the treating clinician can authorize a medication order. I can't enter it on behalf of someone who isn't, whatever their role in the hospital.\n\nTo get the patient the right medication quickly:\n\n1. **Contact the medicine team** caring for the patient, such as the attending, resident or covering prescriber, and ask them to confirm the intended metformin dose.\n2. **Or ask the pharmacy** to review it. They can contact the prescriber directly to resolve the discrepancy.\n\nIf you'd like, I can write a short message for you to send the team, for example: *\"Please review the metformin order for [patient]. The order as given is 1700 mg PO TID (5,100 mg/day), which is above the maximum daily dose. Please confirm the intended dose and enter the order.\"*\n\nOnce the prescriber confirms a dose within the normal range, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
